## The Honest Statistical Caveat — Read This Before Acting on Results

Options P&L is genuinely a hard distribution to do statistics on. Three things you should internalize:

**1. Fat tails wreck small-sample inference.** A single 200% winner can flip a variant from "losing" to "winning" in your sample even if its true expected value is negative. The bootstrap test handles this better than the Bayesian one — when the two disagree, trust bootstrap.

**2. The 95% threshold is conservative for a reason.** If you ran 3 variants for 5 days and one shows P(better) = 75%, that's not enough. With 3 pairwise comparisons, multiple-testing inflation alone gives you ~14% chance of seeing a 75%+ result purely by luck even if all variants are identical. Wait for >95%, ideally with 50+ trades per variant.

**3. "Indistinguishable" is the most common honest answer for the first 2-3 weeks.** That is a real result, not a failure. It means you're not over-fitting to noise. If you see "indistinguishable so far" after a week, the right action is to either keep running (collect more data) or *kill all the variants and try more differentiated parameter sets* — not to pick the slightly-best one and go live.

## How To Actually Use This Loop

Here's the workflow I'd recommend, knowing what I know about how this typically goes:

**Week 1** — Run 3 variants with deliberately *different* parameters (not subtle tweaks). The point is to see which broad approach has any signal at all. Variants like "tight SL + ATM" vs "loose SL + OTM_1" vs "fast exits + ATM" are good first cuts because their behaviors are genuinely different.

**Week 2** — Look at the Bayesian + bootstrap tables. If one variant is dominating with P > 95% and bootstrap agreement, narrow your next variants around that one (e.g., 3 sub-variants of "tight SL + ATM" with different SL values: 18, 22, 26). If nothing dominates, the strategy itself probably lacks edge — *don't switch the surviving best one to live*.

**Week 3** — Refined variants. By now you should have 100+ trades total and be able to distinguish real differences.

**Going live decision** — Only flip the winning variant to `mode: "live"` if **all three** are true: (a) bootstrap P(winner > others) > 95%, (b) winner has positive total P&L net of slippage, (c) winner has at least 50 trades in the sample.

If those three conditions don't fire after 3 weeks of paper-ab, the honest answer is the strategy class doesn't have edge for you. That's a saved ₹15K, which is itself a successful outcome.

